Essentially, I would expect an InterfaceMarshaler to be able to return a customized ast.Node and have that be used. Instead, the code treats it as a normal struct, so far as I can tell from testing and the code in `encodeValue`.

If I could encode the Node myself, then within my `MarshalYAML` function I can call `yaml.ValueToNode` on the values I need encoded as child nodes, and I can add comments and tags and whatever as necessary. Without that capability, it is entirely unclear how to construct a custom node with relevant comments etc. that can be used by the Marshaling process writ large.

Before the check to see if we can encode by Marshaler, if we do the following:

```
	if v.CanInterface() {
		iface := v.Interface()
		if node, ok := iface.(ast.Node); ok {
			return node, nil
		}
	}
```

We preserve the node.

**Describe the bug**

A null yaml anchor `opts: &opts null` fails to unmarshal into a struct, but will work when unmarshalling into `map[any]any`.

**To Reproduce**

Playground example: https://go.dev/play/p/f4UtPY-WvyW

Example code:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"log"

	"github.com/goccy/go-yaml"
)

type Opts struct {
	*Opts `yaml:",omitempty,inline,alias"`
	ID    string `yaml:"id,omitempty"`
	Name  string `yaml:"name,omitempty"`
}

type Product struct {
	Name string
	Opts *Opts
}

type Doc struct {
	DefaultOpts *Opts      `yaml:"opts,anchor"`
	Products    []*Product `yaml:"products"`
}

func main() {
	defaultOpts := &Opts{
		Name: "Default Opts",
		ID:   "default-id",
	}

	doc := &Doc{
		// HERE: Uncomment this line, and it works as expected
		//DefaultOpts: defaultOpts,
	}

	for i := range 2 {
		doc.Products = append(doc.Products, &Product{
			Name: fmt.Sprintf("Product-%d", i),
			Opts: &Opts{
				Opts: defaultOpts,
				ID:   fmt.Sprintf("id-%d", i),
			},
		})
	}

	bytes, err := yaml.Marshal(doc)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}

	fmt.Println(string(bytes))

	// Unmarshal the config
	var got Doc

	err = yaml.Unmarshal(bytes, &got)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	fmt.Println("GOT:", got)
}
```

